{#
  view/j-translate/j-translate/delete.phtml — the confirmation.

  ## The heading is a translated interpolation, and it stays one

  `'Delete ' ~ entity` reaches the translator as the finished string `Delete
  translation-phrase`, which is what the .phtml does and what the phrase table already
  holds. It is the wrong shape — the entity name should be a parameter rather than part of
  the key, which is what `JTranslate\I18n\TranslatableMessage` exists for — but changing it
  here would retire a phrase and file a new one on both the heading and the `<title>`. Filed
  in the host's backlog with the other phrase-shape items instead.

  ## The phrase text is escaped here and was not in the .phtml

  The warning names the phrase so a translator can see *which* one they are about to
  destroy, and the .phtml interpolated it with `sprintf` into HTML with no escaping. Phrase
  text is written by developers rather than by visitors, so nothing was ever exploited —
  but a phrase containing `<` rendered as broken markup, and "the data is trusted" is not a
  property a template should depend on. Twig escapes it.

  Cancel is a `Button` and not a submit: `DeletePhraseForm` declares it as one, because an
  element with no `type` renders as `type="submit"` and clicking Cancel used to delete the
  phrase. The controller also refuses a POST naming it, before the CSRF check, for a
  hand-crafted request or a page cached from before that fix.
#}
{% extends jtranslate_layout %}

{% block content %}
<h1>{{ translate(page_title) }}</h1>
<p>{{ translate('Are you sure you want to delete the phrase "%s" from the translation database? Only delete if you\'re sure this translation is no longer used, otherwise it will reappear the next time the website encounters the same string.')|format(phrase.phrase) }}</p>
{{ form_open(form, form_action) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('security')) }}
{#- The `&nbsp;` is the .phtml's own separator between the two buttons, and it has to sit
    hard against both of them: it decodes to U+00A0, which is not whitespace to a browser
    or to the port harness's normalizer, so a newline either side of it renders as three
    spaces where laminas renders one. -#}
{{ form_submit(form.get('submit')) }}&nbsp;{{ form_button(form.get('cancel')) }}
{{ form_close() }}
{% endblock %}
